 \runin{Beyond reservoirs: an entropy budget.}For other noise, the result becomes an entropy budget~\cite{SM}. Any additional channel with budget $b$ adds $b$ bits, and the upper bound holds with $k$ replaced by $k+b$. Unital noise (Pauli errors, dephasing, coherent errors, crosstalk) and projective measurements whose outcomes only enter the readout~\cite{Mujal2023} have $b=0$; since every unital qubit channel with full-rank Choi matrix contains a depolarizing component, the theorem covers generic independent unital noise~\cite{SM}. Resets of $m$ qubits count like $m$ input qubits. Amplitude damping with probability $0<\gamma<1$ adds $n\log_2(1+\gamma)$ bits, and relaxing idle qubits then act as a refrigerator~\cite{BenOr2013} that lets a reservoir with two input qubits remember for a time exponential in $n$~\cite{SM,Hoeffding1963}. In the language of error correction, a measurement-free recovery that consumes $k$ fresh ancillas per round is, with its data qubits, a system in $\mathcal N$ [Fig.~\ref{fig:scope}]: it keeps a logical bit for at most $\exp[O_q(k)]$ rounds beyond the washout of its initial state, whatever its size, and feedback of syndromes into a classical memory, outside the model, can lift this ceiling~\cite{SM}. Without a depolarizing component or with non-Markovian noise~\cite{Sannia2026}, memory can likewise be unbounded~\cite{SM}.
 
-\runin{Conclusion.}Under the stated noise model, the fresh-qubit supply sets the memory ceiling; reaching its scaling requires dynamics that uses those qubits to remove errors. Fresh qubits are necessary to reach the memory limit but not sufficient; standard reservoirs, which do not use them for repair, operate far below it. The limit bounds how far back a task can reach; how well a reservoir processes the inputs within that range is a separate question~\cite{Cao2026}. Open problems include closing the gap between the two exponents, whether a quantum code can beat the classical rate $\alpha_q$, what limits memory with feedback or non-Markovian noise, and testing the scaling by varying $k$ at fixed $n$ with mid-circuit resets~\cite{Yasuda2023,Hu2024}.
+\runin{Conclusion.}After initialization washout, fresh-qubit supply sets a size-independent memory ceiling for Markovian quantum dynamics with a fixed local depolarizing component, no classical feedback, and no other entropy sink. Fresh qubits are necessary but insufficient: an explicit reservoir uses them for repair and attains exponential scaling, whereas standard reservoirs without repair operate far below the ceiling. The ceiling bounds how far back a task can reach; how well a reservoir processes the inputs within that range is a separate question~\cite{Cao2026}. Open problems include closing the exponent gap, whether a quantum code can beat the classical rate $\alpha_q$, what limits memory with feedback or non-Markovian noise, and testing the scaling by varying $k$ at fixed $n$ with mid-circuit resets~\cite{Yasuda2023,Hu2024}.
 
